 "goshi_ambiguities": {
  "3 canonical form": "[matches the author's, 8 November 2026] Section 3 says the payload is strings, null and nested objects/arrays of the same, amounts as decimal strings, so JCS (RFC 8785) and codepoint-sorted compact JSON coincide. This module uses sorted-key compact JSON, which is JCS over that value domain extended to integers below 2^53 (JCS formats those as plain integers too; the intent scope keeps the decision chain's integer amounts so that its digest is the digest of the same bytes); it refuses a float, a boolean or a non-BMP key rather than claim JCS for it. Xavier Goshi confirmed the reading (his implementation: sorted keys, compact separators, UTF-8, SHA-256).",
  "3 action_hash": "[resolved by the author, 8 November 2026] the text names action_hash without saying what is hashed. Farley's action_ref (F-2.2) hashes {agentId, actionType, scopeRequired, timestamp}, a different thing. Our first reading (16 October to 7 November): the SHA-256 of the executed action's argument bytes as the tool door hashed them, named in the manifest. The author's construction, followed since 8 November: the canonical hash of the executed_action object recorded in the action's ScopeEvaluated ledger event, scope_hash built the same way from intent_scope. The two coincide here because the tool arguments are a sorted-key compact JSON object, so the digest the other three links hash is the same hex (join K1).",
  "3 evidence_set_hash": "not defined in the text. Here: SHA-256 over the canonical JSON of the sorted list of every evidence digest the packet cites (agent anchors, settlement anchor_hash, ledger entry). Re-derivable from the manifest; the construction is this module's.",
  "3 ledger_entry_hash": "[resolved by the author, 8 November 2026; the event shape stays ours] the text speaks of a ledger slice, ledger events and ledger event hashes (4.1, 4.4, 5) without a format. The author's construction, followed since 8 November: ledger_entry_hash is the event_hash of an event of the slice for the same action_id, and when that event carries action_packet_hash it must equal action_hash (here: the ActionExecuted event, which carries it). Still ours: the event shape {seq, action_id, type, at, data, prev_hash, event_hash}, event_hash = SHA-256 of the canonical JSON of the event without event_hash, prev_hash the previous event_hash (genesis: 64 zeros); his implementation's event has more members and a payload_hash, which -01 does not specify either.",
  "3 mandate_hash": "named as the AP2 mandate_hash (4.2). A chain whose mandate is a person's signed approval, not an AP2 mandate, has no source for it: set to null, as 4.2 does for the network token id and the 3DS result when the implementation has no source. Whether null is allowed for mandate_hash is not said.",
  "3 rail_anchors_hash": "a receipt member; the settlement block of 4.3 has its own anchor_hash. The text does not say they are equal. Here rail_anchors_hash = the settlement anchor_hash, which is what lets Section 5's 'headline consistency across manifest, receipt' be checked at all.",
  "3 key_id and signer_pubkey_hash": "key_id is taken as equal to the envelope's kid and the payload's issuer_id (F-2.2: issuer_id MUST match kid); signer_pubkey_hash as SHA-256 of the raw 32-byte Ed25519 public key, hex. Neither construction is in the text.",
  "3 timestamp and issued_at": "the profile lists timestamp and the base draft requires issued_at; both are carried, timestamp read as the time of the decision stated, issued_at as the time the receipt was signed.",
  "4.1 categories": "the CE 3.0 / FPT view is a mapping of what the packet holds into three named categories with gap notes; the text defines no field names. Here: {category: {populated, value_or_null, gap_note}}. The verifier only checks that a category marked populated has a value, that the dispute reason code is present (4.1: a missing one MUST cause the mapper to raise), and that no category is populated from a source the manifest does not hold.",
  "4.2 approval event hash": "[resolved by the author, 8 November 2026; one extension kept, one point open] the text names an 'approval event hash' with no construction and no type. Our first reading: a typed digest {type, alg, digest} of the DPS approval held outside the ledger (A1). The author's construction, followed since 8 November: the event_hash of an ApprovalGranted or ApprovalDenied event of the slice whose payload carries an action_hash equal to the receipt's. Extension beyond -01 (the author calls it reasonable, 'not what -01 meant'): the ApprovalGranted payload also carries dps_approval, the typed digests of A1 and of the signatures attachment, so that the approval the event records can be resolved against the bytes held; a verifier of -01 can ignore that member. Open: when the executed action breaches the approved scope (the stated negative chain: 120.00 approved, 150.00 executed), the approval our chain holds is of the intent scope, so the event's action_hash is the scope_hash and not the receipt's action_hash; this module accepts that pairing only together with a BREACH verdict and names any other mismatch. Also still ours: which field a BREACH names when several differ (the first in sorted order).",
  "4.2 intent scope and executed action": "[resolved by the author, 8 November 2026] what the two look like was not said; now: JSON objects recorded in the action's ScopeEvaluated ledger event (data.intent_scope, data.executed_action, with scope_hash and action_hash recomputed from them). The manifest keeps copies and their sources (which attachment and which receipt member they come from), checked equal to the event's; the sources are ours.",
  "4.2 receipt_signature in agent_anchors": "the block lists 'receipt signature' among agent-native facts: read as the hex signature of the dispute receipt itself, which the block then repeats. A block that carries it must carry the same value as the envelope.",
  "4.3 anchor_hash": "[resolved by the author, 8 November 2026] 'a canonical hash of the anchor content': our first reading was the SHA-256 of the canonical JSON of the block without anchor_hash. The author's construction, followed since 8 November: the block is {status: 'anchored', anchor, anchor_hash} and anchor_hash is the canonical hash of the anchor object alone (unanchored_fields inside it, as a statement about the content); an unanchored packet carries exactly {status: 'SETTLEMENT_UNANCHORED'}, which this module reports under the text's name, and a manifest with no block at all fails under a name of ours (SETTLEMENT_BLOCK_MISSING).",
  "4.3 settlement versus headline": "Section 5 asks for headline consistency; which headline the settlement amount is compared with is not said. Here: manifest.headline.amount_minor, which the manifest states as the amount in dispute; a mismatch is named SETTLEMENT_HEADLINE_MISMATCH (this module's name).",
  "4.4 wrapper": "acquisition record, custody record and authentication statement are described, not specified. Here: acquisition {captured_by, captured_at, tool, clock_source, gaps[]} (gaps declared, custodian never invented: captured_by MUST be present; 7: a host clock MUST be declared untrusted absent an external anchor), custody [{event, at, actor, ledger_event_hash}], authentication_statement {re_derivable: [names of checks]}.",
  "5 failure names": "the text names failures by example. SCOPE_HEADLINE_MISMATCH, SETTLEMENT_ANCHOR_HASH_MISMATCH and SETTLEMENT_UNANCHORED are the text's; every other code this verifier emits is its own.",
  "F-2.1 envelope": "the envelope shape only (payload + signature{alg, kid, sig}); the flat shape of F-2.1 and F-6.6 is refused, not verified, since the profile's receipt is a 'canonical dict' under an envelope."
 },
 "all_verified": true
